 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "Cities: at, to and from",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "With the name of a city, Latin leaves out the preposition. The case on its own says whether you are at the city, going to it, or coming from it."
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Latin",
       "Case",
       "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "<em>Romae</em>",
        "locative",
        "at Rome, in Rome"
       ],
       [
        "<em>Romam</em>",
        "accusative",
        "to Rome"
       ],
       [
        "<em>Roma</em>",
        "ablative",
        "from Rome"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "nuntius <u class=\"c\">Romae</u> est.",
      "en": "The messenger is <b>in Rome</b>.",
      "note": "<em>Romae</em> is locative: \"at\" or \"in Rome\"."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "amici <u class=\"c\">Romam</u> navigant.",
      "en": "The friends sail <b>to Rome</b>.",
      "note": "There is no <em>ad</em>: the accusative alone means \"to\"."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "The <b>locative</b> is a sixth case, used for where something happens with cities, towns and small islands.",
      "For a 1st declension city like <em>Roma</em>, the locative ends in <em>-ae</em>, the same as the genitive.",
      "Rome is the city on the word list. Other cities in passages are glossed, and they work in the same way."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "time",
   "h2": "Time how long",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "To say how long something goes on, Latin puts the length of time in the <b>accusative</b>, again with no preposition. English adds \"for\"."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servi <u class=\"v\">quinque horas</u> laborant.",
      "en": "The slaves work <b>for five hours</b>.",
      "note": "<em>horas</em> is accusative plural: how long they work."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "puer <u class=\"v\">horam</u> dormit.",
      "en": "The boy sleeps <b>for an hour</b>."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "<em>horam</em>, \"for an hour\"; <em>annum</em>, \"for a year\".",
      "<em>quinque</em>, five, and <em>decem</em>, ten, never change their endings: <em>quinque horas</em>, <em>decem annos</em>.",
      "The accusative of time answers the question \"for how long?\" The object of the verb is a separate word."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating them",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "A city with no preposition: look at the ending. <em>-ae</em> is \"at\", <em>-am</em> is \"to\", <em>-a</em> is \"from\".",
      "An accusative of hours or years: add \"for\"."
     ]
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "viri <u class=\"c\">Roma</u> veniunt.",
      "en": "The men come <b>from Rome</b>."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "dominus <u class=\"c\">Romae</u> <u class=\"v\">decem annos</u> laborat.",
      "en": "The master works <b>in Rome for ten years</b>."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "With a verb of being, staying or working, <em>Romae</em> is \"at Rome\" or \"in Rome\", even though it looks like the genitive.",
      "<em>Romam</em> is \"to Rome\" even without <em>ad</em>.",
      "<em>Roma</em> after a verb of coming or sailing is \"from Rome\".",
      "<em>horas</em> and <em>annos</em> say how long: add \"for\", as in \"for five hours\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
